## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical knowledge of securing software supply chains in cloud-native environments, addressing real-world security risks that SaaS platforms face when handling third-party plugins and dependencies.

## Key Concepts

- SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) generation and analysis
- Dependency pinning with cryptographic hashes
- Plugin attestation and private registry management
- Pull request gates for drift detection and reproducible builds
- Incident response protocols for compromised plugins (72-hour SLA)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: Attestation verification for plugin registry
const verifyPluginAttestation = async (pluginId, attestation) => {
  const isValidSignature = await crypto.verify(attestation.signature, publicKey);
  if (!isValidSignature) throw new Error('Invalid plugin attestation');

  const sbom = await generateSBOM(pluginId);
  const hasVulnerabilities = await scanVulnerabilities(sbom);

  return { isValidSignature, hasVulnerabilities, sbom };
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

To assess practical control-plane hardening choices under real-world friction, focusing on mobile app token misuse and multi-tenant isolation.

## Key Concepts

- OAuth2/OIDC security best practices (PKCE, short TTLs)
- Token binding and device fingerprinting
- Token rotation and per-tenant scopes
- Token introspection and revocation
- Observability and IR readiness

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: rotate refresh token on use and bind to device and tenant
function onRefresh(token, deviceId, tenant) {
  if (!isValidDevice(token.bindInfo, deviceId, tenant)) throw new Error('invalid');
  const newToken = issueAccessToken(tenant, token.tenant);
  rotateRefreshToken(token.id);
  return { accessToken: newToken, refreshToken: getRotatedRefresh(token.id) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the token binding mechanism in CI/CD?
- What metrics indicate token abuse and how would you respond?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical knowledge of enforcing strict multi-tenant isolation, combining API gateway ABAC, envelope encryption, tenancy separation in data stores, and incident response readiness in a real-world, globally distributed ride-hailing context.

## Key Concepts

- ABAC policies per tenant at API gateway
- Envelope encryption with per-tenant keys (KMS)
- Service mesh mTLS and SPIRE identities
- Per-tenant data stores and RLS
- IR playbooks with cross-region logs and forensics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example access check
function authorize(user, resource) {
  if (user.tenant_id !== resource.tenant_id) {
    throw new Error('tenant_mismatch');
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate per-tenant keys without downtime?
- What metrics define measurable success criteria for this plan?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate data-security policy into concrete export controls.

## Key Concepts
- Data minimization and redaction
- RBAC and per-session scopes
- Token-based session gating
- Centralized exporting logs and retention
- IR playbooks and containment steps

## Code Example
```javascript
// Express route sketch: enforce role-based redaction on export
app.post('/exports/statements', authorize(['viewer','exporter']), (req,res)=>{
  const fields = req.user.role==='exporter' ? fullFields : ['date','amount','balance'];
  const csv = generateCSV(data, fields);
  res.setHeader('Content-Type','text/csv');
  res.send(csv);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
